narrative:
## Computing Factorials

The factorial of n (written n!) is the product of all positive integers from 1 to n.

- 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120
- 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = 6
- 1! = 1
- 0! = 1 (by definition)

### The Factorial Function

```xiom
fn factorial(n: Int) -> Int {
  if n < 0 {
    return 0;
  }
  var result = 1;
  var i = 1;
  while i <= n {
    result = result * i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Tracing factorial(5)

| i | result | calculation |
|---|--------|-------------|
| 1 | 1      | 1 × 1 = 1  |
| 2 | 2      | 1 × 2 = 2  |
| 3 | 6      | 2 × 3 = 6  |
| 4 | 24     | 6 × 4 = 24 |
| 5 | 120    | 24 × 5 = 120|

The accumulator `result` starts at 1 and grows by multiplying with each `i` from 1 to n.

### Calling the Function

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let f5 = factorial(5);
  let f7 = factorial(7);
  let f0 = factorial(0);

  io.println("Factorials computed!");

  return 0;
}
```

### Why Start at 1?

Starting `result = 1` is the multiplicative identity. Starting at 0 would make every factorial 0 (anything × 0 = 0). This is the same reason `i` starts at 1 — multiplying by 0 would ruin the product.

